x87 at 64-bit precision inline and exact: the x80 itself as the shadow.

Patches 06/37/45 keep the x87 stack in host registers across a TB at
PC=53 (binary64 shadows) and PC=24 (binary32), and patch 47 can run PC=64
at 53 bits, inexactly. Exact PC=64 code still took a helper call per
instruction into softfloat's 80-bit path: 3DMark2001 SE's Lobby spends
over half its vCPU there (FCW=033f, doc 13 "PC=64 as 53 bits").

Mode 3 (x87_fast_mode 3: PC=64, RC nearest, PM masked, x87-fast on; the
TB flag's fourth value) keeps the shadows as the x80 values themselves,
the mantissa in an i64 global and sign | exponent in an i32 one (env
x87_xl[] / x87_xh[]), zero or normal like the other modes' shadows. The
exponent range is the x87's, so nothing falls back for range; reloads and
materializations are copies, fld m32/m64 exact conversions, fild an inline
normalization, compares a 128-bit integer key. +, -, * and / are calls to
pure helpers (TCG_CALL_NO_RWG_SE, the shadows stay in host registers):
x87f_binop_x in x87-fast.h, a 64x64->128 product, a 128-bit aligned sum
with a sticky bit, a 128/64 quotient, rounded to 64 bits nearest-even --
what softfloat computes, without its canonicalization -- returning an "ok"
bit that sends overflow, tininess, denormals and NaNs to the slow block.
fst m32/m64 and fist round through pure helpers of the same kind; fsqrt
and frndint are not inlined in this mode. The unwinder copies x87_xl/xh
back for a mode 3 TB. The PC=64 arithmetic of the ordinary helpers
(x87_fast_prec) takes the same integer path, so code that is not inlined
gains too.

